1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,483
Imagine planning an early Pokémon game.

2
00:00:04,483 --> 00:00:10,617
Mixing movement, battles, and Poké Ball use in one flat list makes a
specific rule hard to find.

3
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:14,800
We will organize those notes into headings and subordinate details.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:21,167
We will start with an illustrative Pokémon document, use game menus
and move descriptions to learn folding and moving, and then prepare

5
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:22,300
a document for a reader.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,300
This is an illustrative planning document based on an early Pokémon
game, not an original document from its developer.

7
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:32,317
Divide the rules into Exploration and Battles.

8
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:35,183
Under Battles, place Move Selection and Item Use.

9
00:00:35,183 --> 00:00:39,683
Put the conditions and results for using a Poké Ball together under
Item Use.

10
00:00:40,133 --> 00:00:42,433
To revise the catching rule, expand that branch.

11
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:48,967
You can find the related conditions and results together, reducing the
risk of mixing them with unrelated rules or overlooking a detail.

12
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:07,067
An outline places smaller items under larger ones to make their
grouping visible.

13
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:12,700
Here, a level shows where an explanation belongs, rather than how
important it is.

14
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,317
Take the menus in Super Smash Bros.

15
00:01:15,317 --> 00:01:15,917
Ultimate.

16
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:19,617
Selecting a main category reveals the choices inside it.

17
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:26,300
The Vault groups related items such as Sounds, Replays, and Records.

18
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:31,667
The parent sets the scope, while its children divide that scope into
more specific parts.

19
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:45,533
Options contains different choices such as sound, brightness, and
language.

20
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:50,417
Inside Sound, the controls distinguish music, sound effects, and voices.

21
00:01:54,433 --> 00:02:01,650
We can organize text the same way: Options at the first level, Sound
below it, and sound-effect volume one level deeper.

22
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:05,050
Indentation makes the relationship visible.

23
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:09,417
Sound and Brightness are peers, while effect volume belongs under
Sound.

24
00:02:09,417 --> 00:02:14,467
This is an analogy for organizing text, not a claim about the game’s
internal code.

25
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:28,717
Now apply the same approach to a fighter’s moves.

26
00:02:28,717 --> 00:02:31,417
The Help menu includes a Move List.

27
00:02:31,417 --> 00:02:35,567
Let us use that subject to build a document structure.

28
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:42,467
In our illustrative document, the levels are Fighter, Special Moves, and
an individual move.

29
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:45,833
Under that move, we write its input, action, and result.

30
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:51,117
How to press the button and what action follows are details of the
same move.

31
00:02:51,117 --> 00:02:58,667
We are illustrating document structure, not recreating measured game
values or Nintendo’s internal design document.

32
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:07,600
When the move list grows, you do not need to read every detail at
once.

33
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:13,283
Collapse the details to scan the headings, then expand them to reveal
the same content.

34
00:03:13,617 --> 00:03:17,467
Collapsing does not delete anything; it changes what is visible.

35
00:03:17,467 --> 00:03:22,033
To change the order, move the heading together with its subordinate
content.

36
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:24,667
Changing the level changes the grouping.

37
00:03:24,667 --> 00:03:28,883
Check whether an item became a peer or moved under another
heading.

38
00:03:28,883 --> 00:03:34,117
Distinguishing order changes from level changes helps avoid
misplaced explanations.

39
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:39,100
You do not need to build a special planning tool to try this.

40
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:44,583
In desktop Word, Outline view under the View tab lets you work with
document levels.

41
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:51,517
Assign the main headings and subheadings, display fewer levels to
inspect the overall structure, and expand the branch you need.

42
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:54,400
The controls may differ by program and version.

43
00:03:54,400 --> 00:04:01,183
The essential step is assigning the relationship between headings and
their content, rather than merely making text larger.

44
00:04:01,917 --> 00:04:05,850
Once the outline has a structure, consider who will read it.

45
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:11,733
Notes for developing ideas and a document handed to someone else
serve different purposes.

46
00:04:12,917 --> 00:04:17,517
Working notes can contain questions that are still unresolved.

47
00:04:17,517 --> 00:04:23,517
A document for someone else should make the decisions and the
reading path clear.

48
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:27,500
For the same move, working notes may separate input and result for
comparison.

49
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,967
A handoff document arranges the headings and explanation for the
reader.

50
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:34,383
The organizing tool and the final presentation need not look identical.

51
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:38,283
This method is not limited to game design documents.

52
00:04:38,283 --> 00:04:43,867
You can add detailed explanations to video topics, or organize study
material and questions.

53
00:04:44,183 --> 00:04:45,950
Try a small list of your own.

54
00:04:45,950 --> 00:04:52,133
Place related items under a heading, expand one branch to inspect its
details, then collapse it again.

55
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:02,650
Can you find the explanation you want from the headings alone?

56
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:07,767
If so, you have more than a long list: you have a structure for finding
and revising ideas.
